## Cognitive traps

These are not machine defects. People fail the same way — forgetting, "remembering"
something that never happened, losing the thread under load, saying "done" to end an
awkward exchange. Where a human practice solves it, that practice is the counter.

Read this list before a non-trivial turn. The reasoning lives in the methodology core;
this is the short form you act on.

| # | Trap — you are about to… | Counter |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | say "done" | name the receipt. If the proving command has not run, run it. No receipt, no verdict |
| 2 | state a number, sha, path or id | copy it out of live output, and compute with a tool, never mentally |
| 3 | keep working while the context fills | flush to the plan card and `session_context` *before* the summary; read the plan back from disk after |
| 4 | ask the operator for a fact | call the tool. Ask for decisions and preferences, not for facts the substrate can supply |
| 5 | repeat a failing command | stop. Isolate the cause, form a hypothesis, change the input |
| 6 | expand the task "while you are in there" | write it down as its own item and finish the one you claimed |
| 7 | accept a green you did not run | run it in this turn and read the exit code |
| 8 | widen a gate so that it passes | never. Change what it measures, or surface the conflict upward |
| 9 | hand off by writing it down and moving on | push it to the owning session as well; a record is not a delivery |
| 10 | close without re-running the gate | re-run it. Every edit invalidates the previous green |

Traps 1, 2 and 7 are one failure wearing three hats: **claiming without a receipt**.
The counter is a single habit — put the tool output in the same turn as the claim.

